## Third-party attribution

This plugin adapts material from four donor projects, all MIT-licensed. Each
paragraph below lists what was adapted from that donor.

- **superpowers © Jesse Vincent (MIT)**: the SessionStart hook pattern (`hooks/hooks.json`,
  `hooks/session-start.js`), git-discipline Parts 1/3, tdd, the systematic-debugging
  scaffold and its vendored supporting files (`root-cause-tracing.md`,
  `defense-in-depth.md`, `condition-based-waiting.md`), the
  review-intake steps in `agents/developer.md`, the plan format and execution loop in
  delegated-implementation, the writing-skills base (`.agents/skills/writing-skills`), the interview skill
  (adapted from brainstorming), and cherry-picks into the core
  (dispatching-parallel-agents, verification-before-completion, brainstorming).

- **agent-skills © 2025 Addy Osmani (MIT)**: commit hygiene in git-discipline (Part 2),
  source-driven-development, and in `library/` api-and-interface-design,
  security-and-hardening and production-readiness (observability and the deploy checklist),
  grafts (TDD, debugging, review, planning, architecture), the project-file template in
  project-onboarding, grafts into the agents (developer, tester), and cherry-picks
  into the core.

- **skills © 2026 Matt Pocock (MIT)**: the merge-conflicts substep of git-discipline,
  Phase 1 of systematic-debugging, the module-boundaries section of the design method
  (`skills/architecture/design-method.md`), to-questionnaire (`library/`), grafts
  (architecture, tdd, review, research-doc format), and
  cherry-picks into the core.

- **ponytail © Dietrich Gebert (MIT)**: the lean-code ladder, the "root cause, not
  symptom" rule, the "never simplify away" carve-outs and the one-check test rule in
  the core, `agents/developer.md`, `agents/tester.md` and tdd; the complexity-review tags
  (`delete / stdlib / native / yagni / shrink`) in `agents/critic.md` and
  `agents/auditor.md`; the one-source-many-adapters packaging pattern.
